  abstract = {Fisher market is an economic model attributed to Irving Fisher. It has the following ingredients: A set of                         m                 \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle m\}     divisible products with pre-specified supplies (usually normalized such that the supply of each good is 1). A set of                         n                 \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle n\}     buyers. For each buyer                         i         =         1         ,         {\dots}         ,         n                 \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle i=1,{\textbackslash}dots ,n\}    , there is a pre-specified monetary budget                                    B                        i                                     \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle B\_\{i\}\}    . Each product                         j                 \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle j\}     has a price                                    p                        j                                     \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle p\_\{j\}\}    ; the prices are determined by methods described below. The price of a bundle of products is the sum of the prices of the products in the bundle. A bundle is represented by a vector                         x         =                    x                        1                             ,         {\dots}         ,                    x                        m                                     \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle x=x\_\{1\},{\textbackslash}dots ,x\_\{m\}\}    , where                                    x                        j                                     \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle x\_\{j\}\}     is the quantity of product                         j                 \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle j\}    . So the price of a bundle                         x                 \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle x\}     is                         p         (         x         )         =                    {$\sum$}                        j             =             1                                   m                                        p                        j                             {$\cdot$}                    x                        j                                     \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle p(x)={\textbackslash}sum \_\{j=1\}{\textasciicircum}\{m\}p\_\{j\}{\textbackslash}cdot x\_\{j\}\}    . A bundle is affordable for a buyer if the price of that bundle is at most the buyer's budget. I.e, a bundle                         x                 \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle x\}     is affordable for buyer                         i                 \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle i\}     if                         p         (         x         )         {$\leq$}                    B                        i                                     \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle p(x){\textbackslash}leq B\_\{i\}\}    . Each buyer has a preference relation over bundles, which can be represented by a utility function. The utility function of buyer                         i                 \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle i\}     is denoted by                                    u                        i                                     \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle u\_\{i\}\}    .  The demand set of a buyer is the set of affordable bundles that maximize the buyer's utility among all affordable bundles, i.e.:                                                 Demand                                   i                             (         p         )         :=         arg         ⁡                    max                        p             (             x             )             {$\leq$}                            B                                i                                                                    u                        i                             (         x         )                 \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle \{{\textbackslash}text\{Demand\}\}\_\{i\}(p):={\textbackslash}arg {\textbackslash}max \_\{p(x){\textbackslash}leq B\_\{i\}\}u\_\{i\}(x)\}    . A competitive equilibrium (CE) is a price-vector                                    p                        1                             ,         {\dots}         ,                    p                        m                                     \{{\textbackslash}displaystyle p\_\{1\},{\textbackslash}dots ,p\_\{m\}\}    in which it is possible to allocate, to each agent, a bundle from his demand-set, such that the total allocation exactly equals the supply of products. The corresponding prices are called market-clearing prices.   The main challenge in analyzing Fisher markets is finding a CE.:{$\mkern1mu$}103--105},
